The mutated code is in `diag_matmul_kernel` (the non-optimized version), but with M=4096, we always use `diag_matmul_kernel_optimized`.

The mutation is in line 21, which is inside `diag_matmul_kernel`:
```cpp
__global__ void diag_matmul_kernel(
    const float* diag, 
    const float* matrix, 
    float* output, 
    int N, 
    int M
) {
    int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    int row = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;

    if (row < N && col < M) {
        int matrix_idx = row / M + col;  // MUTATION HERE - was row * M + col
        output[matrix_idx] = diag[row] * matrix[matrix_idx];
    }
}
```

But this kernel is NEVER called when M >= 1024. Since our test case has M=4096, the mutated kernel never executes. The optimized version handles all the computation instead, so the mutation has no observable effect on the output.

The fixed input dimensions mean the mutation sits in unreachable code. With N=4096 and M=4096, the condition M >= 1024 always routes execution to the optimized kernel, leaving the mutated kernel untouched. This creates a fundamental coverage problem—the mutation cannot be triggered regardless of how we structure our tests, making it impossible to detect.

The empirical evidence confirms this: across 112 rounds of testing, outputs remain bitwise identical, and all survival metrics show the mutation has no observable effect. The mutated code path is simply never executed given the fixed configuration.

